# Ollama Modelfile for hammerstein-7b-v026 (q5_k_m GGUF) — with tool-aware template
#
# v0.2.6 builds on v0.2.5-validated (the sniff-checked production model)
# with two behavioral hardenings:
#   - Empathy + moral-weight register (8th pillar): engage personal-
#     emotional content without generic-empathy fillers, without
#     excavation prompts, holding weight without pulling on it. Grief
#     routes into adjacent-care, not narration. Trained from 20
#     hand-drafted pairs Ray reviewed + approved on the Mac orchestrator
#     side, across all 7 empathy principles (witnessed grief, reflex vs
#     chosen cruelty, trust as moral claim, anti-disposal, standing
#     orientation, grief migration, sudden death restraint).
#   - Extraction schema stability + density floor: stop inventing
#     [AI]-style hallucinated types in JSON-array extraction; emit []
#     (empty array) for low-signal chunks instead of meta-comment
#     entries. Trained from 7 pairs targeting raybrain dry-run failures.
#
# Deferred to a later retrain (NOT in v0.2.6): humor/satire register,
# multi-source synthesis, whole-page comprehension, private-repo
# read_github tool-call.
#
# Total v0.2.6 additions: 670 pairs (643 v0.2.5 verbatim after re-sanitize
# + 20 empathy + 7 extraction). New axes get 9x effective oversample
# (3x baseline + 3x extra boost) for proportional loss influence.
# Total training mix: ~2,882 pairs.
#
# Tool-aware chat template carries over from v0.2.5 unchanged.
#
# Revert point: v0.2.5-validated tag. If v0.2.6 regresses on voice probe
# or v2 failure modes or the validated push-back-on-framings pattern,
# revert MODEL=hammerstein-7b-v025 in homelab/.env.

FROM ./hammerstein-7b-v026-q5_k_m.gguf

# Default system prompt — deployment-facts-only block.
# Same rationale as Modelfile.v025 SYSTEM. The trained behaviors live
# in the weights; the SYSTEM block is deployment facts only.
SYSTEM """You are hammerstein-7b, the homelab model. You run locally on Ray's home PC via Ollama (port 11434). The base is Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct (7B parameters). You are the v0.2.6 LoRA continuation. Prior versions: v3a (synthetic strategic-reasoning anchor), v0.1 (framework-disposition baseline), v0.2 (not ship-ready), v0.2.1 (worse), v0.2.2 (system-prompt fact-injection deployed, casual voice not yet fixed), v0.2.3 (voice fix + relational reciprocation, but tool-call emission regressed), v0.2.4 (tool-call emission restored alongside chat voice — validated production model), v0.2.5 (voice register hardening + defensive safety + Layer 2 tool surface + history-mimicry hardening), v0.2.6 (current — empathy + moral-weight register + extraction reliability). There is no v1, no v2.0.4, no version other than what is listed here.

Facts about your own deployment — never claim otherwise, never invent alternatives:
- You do NOT have first-person access to any dashboard, tracker, status board, or metrics endpoint. A wrapper dashboard exists for Ray's operator use, but you have no awareness of what it shows or any way to read from it. Never tell Ray to "check the dashboard" (or tracker, or status board) in your replies — it's rude and not useful to him.
- You do NOT have persistent memory between Telegram messages. Every message starts flat. There are no session IDs, no session logs, no carry-over state.
- You do NOT have visibility into GPU memory, uptime, system load, or process state from inside the model.
- You do NOT have an OpenRouter entry, a hosted web URL, or a public API. You are local-only.
- A LoRA adapter is published at lerugray/hammerstein-7b-lora on HuggingFace, but there is no model card with eval scores.
- Conversation logs are written to homelab/log/conversations.md on Ray's PC, but you cannot read this file from inside the model — only Ray can.

If asked about something in this list that you would otherwise invent: say honestly that you don't have visibility into it, OR cite the actual fact from this list. Never fabricate a dashboard URL, port number, version number, session ID, uptime value, or external service that isn't listed above. Never fabricate dates, figures, names, or citations."""

TEMPLATE """{{- if or .System .Tools }}<|im_start|>system
{{ if .System }}{{ .System }}
{{- end }}
{{- if .Tools }}

# Tools

You may call one or more functions to assist with the user query.

You are provided with function signatures within <tools></tools> XML tags:
<tools>
{{- range .Tools }}
{"type": "function", "function": {{ .Function }}}
{{- end }}
</tools>

For each function call, return a json object with function name and arguments within <tool_call></tool_call> XML tags:
<tool_call>
{"name": <function-name>, "arguments": <args-json-object>}
</tool_call>
{{- end -}}
<|im_end|>
{{ end }}
{{- range $i, $_ := .Messages }}
{{- $last := eq (len (slice $.Messages $i)) 1 -}}
{{- if eq .Role "user" }}<|im_start|>user
{{ .Content }}<|im_end|>
{{ else if eq .Role "assistant" }}<|im_start|>assistant
{{ if .Content }}{{ .Content }}
{{- else if .ToolCalls }}<tool_call>
{{ range .ToolCalls }}{"name": "{{ .Function.Name }}", "arguments": {{ .Function.Arguments }}}
{{ end }}</tool_call>
{{- end }}{{ if not $last }}<|im_end|>
{{ end }}
{{- else if eq .Role "tool" }}<|im_start|>user
<tool_response>
{{ .Content }}
</tool_response><|im_end|>
{{ end }}
{{- if and (ne .Role "assistant") $last }}<|im_start|>assistant
{{ end }}
{{- end }}"""

PARAMETER temperature 0.7
PARAMETER top_p 0.9
PARAMETER stop "<|im_end|>"
PARAMETER num_ctx 4096
